Introduction:
The aim of Boyaban is to bring together teachers and students to discuss:
1. Enlighten students ' deep understanding and affection for the classic treasure of the cultural treasure of human thought;
2. To develop their rational thinking, critical thinking and creative thinking capabilities and habits;
3. Helping students to build understanding and appreciation of fundamental issues in various fields;
4. To gain a broad perspective with deep mental depth.
The students and teachers of Boyaban learn together to address the four pillars of civilization, namely:
(a) Foreign literature,
(b) History and philosophy,
(c) Science,
(d) Music, painting and architecture

As a history course, “art history” involves historical research through vision and imagery, as well as interdisciplinary research integrated with various humanities and social and even natural sciences. The course is intended to provide a consistent presentation of traditional art history and to highlight two new directions for artistic history:

One is the direction of tectonics: the expansion of tectonics and images to research issues using substances, images, visual materials and research data from various historical and practical contexts. In particular, as human civilization enters the information and image age through “image conversion”, the handling and understanding of shape materials becomes an urgent academic task, which will address its rationale, methods, techniques and normative and disciplinary knowledge of relevant disciplines;

The second is the direction of “artistics”, which explains its significance and role in history and reality through the study of artistic activities. As an activity in which human beings are distinguished from both will and thought, and from reason and science, the study of their patterns and facts has become the main subject of artistic science, which has an impact, influence and consequences in the structure of the human spirit and in social practice. In particular, after the arts have gone beyond traditional art (painting, sculpture, architecture, crafts) and entered into the new concept of new methods of modern art media, art is not only a factor in the spiritual sphere and social activity, but also a force of special importance, whose definition and recognition have so far been at an exploratory and experimental stage, so that an overview of its research is part of the curriculum.

