Introduction:
1. A holistic understanding of the experimental pathways of ecological research, the rationale of experimental design, characteristics of experimental data, statistical analysis and scientific interpretation of experimental results (classroom teaching);
2. Experimental designs at different levels of plant individuals, populations, communities and ecosystems: These include, inter alia, competitive experiments, density control experiments (experimental stocks), community biodiversity-productivity experiments, global change control experiments, and, in particular, classic ecological experiments (classroom lectures) that have a significant impact on the theory and methodology of ecology.
3 Basic operating techniques (experimentals) in experimental ecology: mainly laboratory development of plant materials, seed growth and reproduction of plants under different moisture and temperature conditions, nutrient growth and reproduction distribution, plant production, DNA extraction, chlorophyll content determination, photocoscopying, evaporation, leaf morphological characteristics, leaf area index, leaf area, distribution of carbon nitrogen in plants, plant competitive experiments, and statistical processing of collected data.

