The course selects representative constitutional cases from China, the United States or Europe for systematic study, all of which address important subjects in the Constitution and political philosophy. Through detailed study of these cases and extensive literature, the curriculum seeks to develop three basic competencies for law students:
1. Legal logic and mental competence: to develop legal and constitutional thinking through the preparation of case summaries and mastery of legal interpretation and logic of legal reasoning, to develop the capacity to study constitutional jurisprudence and to develop its legal analysis and reasoning;
2. Constitutional capacity to reflect on political issues: curriculum reading the literature of relevant cases, discussing the political issues behind the cases and developing the capacity to translate political issues into legal issues for reflection and analysis, thereby fostering statesmen ' s thinking
3. Organization of course panel discussions and presentation of course reports through discussions on expression and communication skills;

The course will examine in detail the 10 classic United States constitutional jurisprudence and discuss its political and legal aspects, thereby giving students an understanding of how lawmakers think in common law countries. The subject seeks to upgrade students in the following areas:
1. Capacity to study English jurisprudence (with Chinese translations) and to read Chinese academic literature in English;
2. Preparation of case digests and book reports and training in basic writing skills;
3. Training in the interpretation of laws and in legal reasoning;
4. Basic capacity for public expression and legal debate;
5. Learning to translate political issues into legal thinking, understanding that law is “political art”

This course is a further systematic introduction of basic knowledge of linear algorithms based on a general analysis of the undergraduate studies. The main elements are as follows:
Chapter I Exchange Banach algebra
1. Algebra preparation knowledge;
2. Gelfand for exchange of Banach algebra;
3 Examples and applications;
4. Gelfand for exchange of C* algebra;
5. Normal algorithmic spectra decomposition theorem and applications in Hilbet space.

Chapter II. The Unbridled Count
1. Concepts of algorithm, model, terminal, co-calculator, symmetry, companionship;
2. spectral analysis and application of Cayley ' s transformation and self-accompaniment algorithms;
3. Deficit index for closed symmetrical algorithms and companion expansion, the Von Neuman guidelines and the expansion of Friedrichs;
4. The disturbance of the self-accompanied algorithm and its spectra under the disturbance;
5. Compulsiveness of unbridled algorithms, precalculated algorithms and condensedness under graphics.

Chapter three. Counts.
1. Definition and nature of infinitum, Hille-Yosida Theorem;
2. Examples of infinity generation units;
3. The Stone Theorem of the Single Parameter Calculus and its application;
4 The Trotter Achievement formula and the Feyman-Kac formula.

