The course focuses on the generation, development and development of the main linguistic schools and on the theory at the frontier. It focuses on the presentation and interpretation of the relevant linguistic theories and research methods. It is designed to help students develop a comprehensive and systematic linguistic knowledge system that will enrich their understanding of the main developments and important points of view of the various linguistic schools and develop their capacity to practice linguistic theory.

The curriculum is based on the existing curriculum and is designed to provide a systematic picture of the development and main achievements of the various linguistic streams and to complement the gaps in the theoretical presentation of the existing curriculum with a view to creating a complete knowledge system of linguistic theory for students. At the same time, the course will be based on the relevant undergraduate courses and will further explore and introduce cutting-edge hot spots in the various streams. The curriculum requires students to have basic knowledge of language and a certain level of foreign language.

Teaching objectives: Through the study of this subject, students are able to gain a full understanding of the dynamics of the generation and development of the main streams of linguistics and have a deeper understanding of the theoretical issues at hand, while at the same time they exercise and raise the theoretical level of their studies.

Teaching requirements:
(1) Complete reading of relevant articles and bibliography chapters;
(2) Careful preparation and conduct of two cross-cutting thematic discussions (including panel cooperation, oral reports);
(3) capable of preparing a complete research programme (including research issues, theoretical frameworks and research design) taking into account their research interests and the views of the relevant theoretical schools of study;
(4) Performing the work required by the teacher in other classes.

Performance technology is one of the core subjects in technical subjects of education. Performance techniques are the systematic process of identifying and analysing performance gaps, planning performance improvement plans, designing and developing problem solutions that reduce gaps, are cost-effective and assess results. The purpose of the course is to promote students ' knowledge of performance in the organization and to master the technical methodological theory of education to improve performance. Performance techniques are applied not only to business organizations but also to school organizations.
Teaching objectives:
1. Students have basic knowledge of performance and technical models and methodologies for performance;
2 Students involved from an empirical point of view in the technical practice of performance and preparing technical analysis of performance;
3. mastery of school performance techniques;
4. Understanding and exploring knowledge points on organizational learning, organizational performance, performance management, etc.

Light co-operation is called “the most important chemical reaction on Earth”. Research for photocosy has been a hot spot for biological and ecological research. The course will focus on the basics of the optical co-operation process, deepen the students ' understanding of the optical co-operation and its methods of research, in conjunction with experiments, internships, etc., and present the latest developments in light co-operation research in the form of lectures.

The objectives of the course are:
1 Understanding the basic principles currently known for light cooperation.
2. Common research methods for light cooperation, including but not limited to:
(1) The basic principles of the PHG experiment are mastered and, depending on the requirements of the experiment, a portable photocolumn instrument (Li-6400 or Li-6800) is used to determine the speed of PVC and the conductivity of the air hole;
(2) mastering the theory of chlorophyll fluorescent analysis and mastering the collection of parameters;
(3) The method of collecting the parameters associated with the gas hole of the leaf;
(4) Knowledge of the methods of extraction and determination of leaf chlorophyll and carrot-likes.
3 To understand the current advance of light cooperation.

Through this course of study, students are able to
